IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `types`
IMPORT `chapter-4-exceptions`

§ `Chapter 16 -- Sexual offences: the general provisions`

-- Penal Code 1871 ss 377C, 377CA, 377CB, 377D.
-- Source: registers/source-bundle/PC1871.txt.
--
-- These four sections govern the whole of ss 375 to 377BO and are encoded first
-- because every offence in the group depends on at least one of them:
--
--   s 377C   interpretation -- what "sexual" means, what a "private act" is, and
--            the definitions of child abuse material, distribute, image and material
--   s 377CA  when a relationship with a minor is exploitative
--   s 377CB  when a consent given under a misconception is not a consent
--   s 377D   when a mistake as to age is a defence, and when it is not
--
-- s 377C's definitions of "child abuse material", "distribute", "image", "material",
-- "structure", "touching" and "vagina" are terms of art used inside the offence
-- sections. They are not decided here: each is carried as part of the fact the
-- offence section asks for, in the module where that section lives. What IS decided
-- here is the two tests in s 377C(3) that have a factual structure -- paragraph (d)
-- on when something is sexual, and paragraph (f) on when a person is doing a private
-- act -- because those two are conditions that other sections conjoin.

§§ `Section 377C(3)(d) -- When penetration, touching, communication or activity is sexual`

-- Penetration, touching, communication or other activity is "sexual" if (i) because
-- of its nature it is sexual, whatever its circumstances or any person's purpose in
-- relation to it may be; or (ii) because of its nature it may be sexual and because
-- of its circumstances or the purpose of any person in relation to it (or both) it
-- is sexual.
--
-- Limb (i) is unconditional; limb (ii) is the two-stage test. They are alternatives,
-- and the second already carries its own conjunction in the fact itself.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 377C(3)(d)"
@export Decide whether an act is sexual within the meaning of Penal Code 1871 section 377C(3)(d)
GIVEN c IS A `Sexual Context` @desc Whether the act is sexual by its nature alone, or may be sexual by its nature and is sexual on its circumstances or a person's purpose.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the act is sexual` IF
        c's `it is sexual because of its nature whatever its circumstances or any person purpose in relation to it`
    OR  c's `it may be sexual because of its nature and is sexual because of its circumstances or the purpose of any person in relation to it or both`

§§ `Section 377C(3)(f) -- Doing a private act`

-- A person is doing a private act if, under circumstances in which the person has a
-- reasonable expectation of privacy, the person (i) is in a state where the genital
-- region, buttocks or breasts (if female) are exposed or covered only in underwear;
-- (ii) is using a toilet, showering or bathing; or (iii) is doing a sexual act not
-- of a kind ordinarily done in public.
--
-- The reasonable expectation of privacy is a conjunct on all three limbs, not a
-- fourth alternative. The Illustration turns on exactly that: a person showering in
-- an open-concept cubicle cannot reasonably expect not to be casually observed, but
-- can reasonably expect not to be surreptitiously recorded -- so the same physical
-- state is or is not a private act according to what is expected of the
-- circumstances.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 377C(3)(f)"
@export Decide whether a person is doing a private act within the meaning of Penal Code 1871 section 377C(3)(f)
GIVEN c IS A `Sexual Context` @desc A reasonable expectation of privacy, together with one of the three states in s 377C(3)(f)(i) to (iii).
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is doing a private act` IF
        c's `the person is in circumstances in which the person has a reasonable expectation of privacy`
    AND (   c's `the person is in a state where the genital region buttocks or breasts if female are exposed or covered only in underwear`
         OR c's `the person is using a toilet showering or bathing`
         OR c's `the person is doing a sexual act that is not of a kind ordinarily done in public`)

§§ `Section 377CA -- Meaning of exploitative relationship`

-- s 377CA(1): whether an accused's relationship with a person below 18 is
-- exploitative is determined by the court in the circumstances of each case, having
-- regard to the minor's age, the age difference, the nature of the relationship and
-- the degree of control or influence exercised. That is a judgment, not a test with
-- limbs, so it is carried as the court's conclusion, asserted.
--
-- s 377CA(2): seven relationships raise a presumption, until the contrary is proved,
-- that the relationship is exploitative -- parent or step-parent, guardian or foster
-- parent; the de facto partner of one of those; teaching or management staff of the
-- minor's school; an established personal relationship formed in giving religious,
-- sporting, musical or other instruction; a custodial officer of an institution
-- where the minor is detained; a doctor, TCM practitioner or psychologist treating
-- the minor; an advocate and solicitor or counsellor acting for the minor.
--
-- The presumption is rebuttable, so `the contrary has been proved so that the
-- presumption does not stand` defeats it. s 377CA(3) removes the presumption
-- altogether for a person lawfully married to the minor, even where the relationship
-- would otherwise fall within one of the seven -- so that is a second, independent
-- defeater and not merely an eighth relationship.
--
-- The court's own determination under subsection (1) is disjoined with the
-- presumption, because a relationship may be found exploitative without falling into
-- any of the seven.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 377CA(2) and (3)"
GIVEN c IS A `Sexual Context`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a section 377CA(2) relationship is presumed exploitative` IF
        (   c's `the accused is the parent step-parent guardian or foster parent of the minor`
         OR c's `the accused is the de facto partner of the parent guardian or foster parent of the minor`
         OR c's `the accused is a member of the teaching or management staff of the school or educational institution at which the minor is a student`
         OR c's `the accused has an established personal relationship with the minor in connection with the provision of religious sporting musical or other instruction to the minor`
         OR c's `the accused is a custodial officer of an institution in which the minor is detained`
         OR c's `the accused is a registered medical practitioner a registered traditional Chinese medicine practitioner or a psychologist and the minor is a patient of the accused`
         OR c's `the accused is an advocate and solicitor or a counsellor and the minor is a client of the accused`)
    AND (NOT c's `the contrary has been proved so that the presumption does not stand`)
    AND (NOT c's `the accused is lawfully married to the minor`)

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 377CA"
@export Decide whether the accused's relationship with a minor is exploitative within the meaning of Penal Code 1871 section 377CA
GIVEN c IS A `Sexual Context` @desc Either the court's determination under s 377CA(1), or one of the seven presumed relationships in s 377CA(2) that has not been rebutted and is not a lawful marriage.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the relationship is exploitative of the minor` IF
        c's `the court determines the relationship to be exploitative having regard to the age of the minor the age difference the nature of the relationship and the degree of control or influence`
    OR  `a section 377CA(2) relationship is presumed exploitative` c

§§ `Section 377CB -- Consent given under misconception in sexual offences`

-- "Despite section 90(a)(ii), a consent for the purposes of an act which is the
-- physical element of a sexual offence is not a consent given by a person under a
-- misconception of fact ONLY IF it is directly related to (a) the nature of the act,
-- namely that it is not of a sexual nature; (b) the purpose of the act, namely that
-- it is not for a sexual purpose; or (c) the identity of the person doing the act,
-- and the person doing the act knows, or has reason to believe, that the consent was
-- given in consequence of such misconception."
--
-- The "only if" is the whole point of the section: it NARROWS s 90(a)(ii) for sexual
-- offences. Under s 90 any misconception of fact can vitiate consent; under s 377CB
-- only these three can. Illustration (d) is the case the narrowing decides -- a
-- misconception as to the attributes of the person (that he is an influential movie
-- director) is not a misconception as to his identity, and the consent stands.
-- Illustrations (a) to (c) are the three limbs made out.
--
-- Because the section is an "only if", this rule is the complete test of when a
-- misconception vitiates consent in a sexual offence, and `the consent is not a
-- consent within section 90` in chapter-4-exceptions.l4 must NOT be used for that
-- purpose. The two are kept separate and this one is the one the sexual offence
-- sections use.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 377CB(1)"
@export Decide whether a consent to a sexual act is vitiated by misconception under Penal Code 1871 section 377CB
GIVEN c IS A `Sexual Context` @desc A misconception directly related to the nature of the act, its purpose, or the identity of the person doing it, known to that person or such that he has reason to believe it. No other misconception vitiates consent to a sexual act.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the consent is vitiated by misconception under section 377CB` IF
        (   c's `the misconception is directly related to the nature of the act namely that it is not of a sexual nature`
         OR c's `the misconception is directly related to the purpose of the act namely that it is not for a sexual purpose`
         OR c's `the misconception is directly related to the identity of the person doing the act`)
    AND c's `the person doing the act knows or has reason to believe that the consent was given in consequence of such misconception`

§§ `Section 377D -- Mistake as to age in sexual offences`

-- s 377D(1): subject to subsections (2) and (3), and despite s 79, a reasonable
-- mistake as to the age of a person cannot be a defence to any charge for a sexual
-- offence. The default is therefore that the defence is NOT available, which is why
-- this rule decides when it is.
--
-- s 377D(2): a reasonable mistaken belief that a minor was of or above 18 IS a valid
-- defence where the fact that the minor is of or above 16 but below 18 is a physical
-- element of the offence. So the defence is confined to the 16-to-18 offences --
-- ss 376AA, 376EA, 376EC, 376EE and 377BL -- and is never available where the
-- element is that the victim was below 16 or below 14.
--
-- s 377D(3): even there the defence is lost if at the time of the offence the
-- accused (a) had previously been charged in court for one of the listed offences,
-- or (b) failed to take all reasonable steps to verify that the minor was of or
-- above 18. Both are defeaters, so both are negated conjuncts.
--
-- The Explanation to s 377D(3) provides that observing the minor participating in
-- activities restricted to persons of or above 18 -- smoking, or admission to a
-- nightclub -- is neither a reasonable basis for the belief nor a reasonable step to
-- verify. It tells a court what does not count towards facts already carried here,
-- and adds no limb of its own.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 377D"
@export Decide whether a mistake as to age is a defence to a sexual offence under Penal Code 1871 section 377D
GIVEN c IS A `Sexual Context` @desc A reasonable mistaken belief that the minor was of or above 18, on an offence whose physical element is that the minor is of or above 16 but below 18, where the accused has no prior charge and took all reasonable steps to verify.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a mistake as to age is a defence` IF
        c's `the accused had a reasonable mistaken belief that the minor was of or above 18 years of age`
    AND c's `the fact that the minor is of or above 16 but below 18 years of age is a physical element of the offence`
    AND (NOT c's `the accused has previously been charged in court for an offence listed in section 377D(3)(a)`)
    AND (NOT c's `the accused failed to take all reasonable steps to verify that the minor was of or above 18 years of age`)

§§ `The spouse carve-outs`

-- ss 375(4), 376(5)(a), 376A(4), 376B(3), 376EB(4), 376ED(4) and 376G each carve out
-- an act against a spouse with that spouse's consent. The wording differs slightly
-- between them but the two facts are the same everywhere, so the pair is gathered
-- here and conjoined by each section that has the carve-out. Sections without one --
-- s 375(1)(a) and (1A)(a), s 376(2) on the non-consent limb, s 376F, s 377, s 377B
-- -- do not call it.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, ss 375(4), 376(5)(a), 376A(4), 376B(3), 376EB(4) and 376ED(4)"
@export Decide whether the spouse carve-out in the Penal Code 1871 sexual offences applies
GIVEN c IS A `Sexual Context` @desc The other person is the accused's spouse and consented. Not every sexual offence has this carve-out -- see the section in question.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the spouse carve-out applies` IF
        c's `the other person is the spouse of the accused`
    AND c's `the spouse consented`
